.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1250 PM PDT Mon Aug 31 2026
(Monday through Friday)

Upper-level shortwave troughing will swing into Northern California
tomorrow; this will result in temperatures well below normal, a
chance for drizzle/light rain, and widespread cloud cover. The best
chances for drizzle/light rain will be in the North Bay,
particularly along the coast and in the higher terrain with the help
of orographic lift. A reinforcing upper-level low and an attendant
cold front will approach the area Wednesday into Thursday. This will
keep well below normal temperatures and cloudy conditions around and
likely make the marine layer arbitrary. There will be a better
chance for drizzle/light rain with this round with a lifting
mechanism in place as PWAT climbs to near one inch (90th
percentile). It is likely (60+% chance) that the northwestern half
of the North Bay gets wetting rain (0.10 inches) between now and
then. While rainfall is expected to be beneficial, wet roadways will
create slick driving conditions and increase stopping distance.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1250 PM PDT Mon Aug 31 2026
(Saturday through Sunday)

Global ensemble clusters diverge on solutions dramatically going
into the weekend, specifically on low vs trough and the location and
timing of the feature, so basically everything. What is certain is
that the region will be under the influence of some kind of
troughing through the weekend. As it weakens through the weekend, a
gradual warming and drying trend will commence with temperatures
moderating to slightly below to near normal. Beyond the seven day
forecast and into Labor Day/early next week, Invest EP95 well
offshore of Southwestern Mexico becomes a variable. Uncertainty over
the weekend will compound and propagate into the tropical side of
the forecast, making it impossible to talk specific impacts at this
point in time. The primary impacts would likely be rain and wind
with impacts likely decreasing from south to north.

